A||||12||Coop88||Suspension soft on one side||||5||
Z||000000||Coop88||06-26-2001||05:08 AM||||I recently replaced all my bushings, endlinks, and rear sway bar brackets with poly, replaced the shocks and struts, but I left my front sway bar brackets alone. The car also has had all 4 balljoints replaced. Car is a formula. I can drive in a straight line and swerve to the left, then to the right, and it feels like the passenger side gives more. Could this be because I didn't replace the front sway bar brackets with new ones?<P>I also hear a noise from the front, loudest at 40mph. It's sort of a "wobbling" noise. It doesn't rattle, and it doesn't thump.  It doesn't go away when the brakes are applied. I can't find anything loose on the front. The car has been making this noise ever since a meeting with a curb. The car tracks straight and I get no vibrations on the interstate or while braking. I'm just wondering what this could be.<BR>Thanks<P>Coop||||reg||5
Z||000001||rogergarrison||06-26-2001||01:00 PM||||maybe a broken coil. Also just simply might be with all the weight on drivers side (driver and no passenger) maybe the left side just settled down more on shocks. Try it with both seats filled. You could also still have a defective shock or strut on the right side - new doesnt mean works<P>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 06-26-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 06-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||84Bill||06-26-2001||01:21 PM||||Did you have an alignment done? If the car is "crabbing" down the road it will react differently to directional changes. It will feel as if one side is slow to react and the other is snappy and responsive when you cut the wheel back and forth several times when cruising at about 45 or so.<P>The noise is probably a wheel bearing<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-26-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000003||Coop88||06-26-2001||04:44 PM||||How would I check to see if it's the wheel bearings (They're $120 each)? Or the shocks, for that matter? Yes, the suspension feels soft on one side with one person and with two people in the car. I did have an alignment done. Thanks for the help so far.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||06-26-2001||04:49 PM||||87ECO5 and I had this same discussion just the other day. <P>I have noticed the same thing on EVERY fiero I have ever been in. I think it is natural. Even my Formula with all new suspension pieces does it. My '86SE does it, WTIII coupe does it, 87eco5 coupe does it...||||reg||1
Z||000005||Will||06-26-2001||10:09 PM||||I don't know if the '88's are like this, but the front bar on the early cars was twisted a little bit from the factory to compensate for the driver's weight and keep the car level. Is you bar installed the right way?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Coop88||06-26-2001||11:31 PM||||I think the bar is mounted correctly. It was easy getting the endlinks on. Could one bracket be more worn than the other? Maybe I should just replace them. Does anyone know a way I can check my wheel hubs without replacing them with new ones (for that noise)? <P>While I was at work, I realized that I haven't noticed the "soft problem" until after I did all this work. I wonder what I could have possibly screwed up. Maybe it's the new shocks like mentioned above. I can't really feel a difference from right to left with the bounce test. Tire pressure is at 34psi on both front tires.<p>[This message has been edited by Coop88 (edited 06-27-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000007||84Bill||06-27-2001||08:18 AM||||If the suspension is softer on one side it will cause the car to "roll" when you hit dips in the road instead of bouncing. My 88 had a definate "roll" and upon closer inspection I found the tire camber (top and bottom) angle was off by a bit. The right rear tire was splayed out at the bottom and was just barely noticable when looking from behind. Over large dips on the the highway it rocked back and forth very violently.||||reg||14
Z||000008||Coop88||07-01-2001||02:55 PM||||I don't know how I missed this before, but when I grab my front wheels at 3 and 9 o'clock, I can wiggle both of them. This is with the car up on jackstands. How much play is ok, and how much is too much? I think this could be causing that noise I hear when I'm rolling? What do you think?||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||07-01-2001||07:26 PM||||Did you check suspension bushings? Those can make the car do wierd things over bumps when they are bad.||||reg||1
Z||000010||mrfiero||07-01-2001||07:50 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Coop88:<BR><B>I don't know how I missed this before, but when I grab my front wheels at 3 and 9 o'clock, I can wiggle both of them. This is with the car up on jackstands. How much play is ok, and how much is too much? I think this could be causing that noise I hear when I'm rolling? What do you think?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Any movement is bad.  You need a helper to do this, but put it back up on jackstands and have your friend grab the wheel at 3 & 9 and try to wiggle it while you are underneath the car with a flashlight.  You need to look at the balljoints, tie rod end and inner bushing area.  You should not see any movement at all in these areas......if you do then that particular part is defective or not torqued down good.  <P>Also look at the upper control arm bushings while wiggling the wheel......sometimes the nuts back off just enough to cause problems.  You can remedy this (if they are loose) by zapping the 2 nuts with an impact wrench.  <P>Generally speaking side to side movement (3 & 9 o'clock) indicates bad bearings while top to bottom (6 & 12 o'clock) is bad balljoints.....usually the lower.<P>Since you just replaced everything, I would almost guarantee you have a bad wheel bearing.  I have a few good used '88 wheel bearings, so you won't have to fork over big $$ for a new one (unless you want to).  <P>I will be in your area next week sometime and can stop by to take a look.  I know that's a ways away, but LMK.<P>  <p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 07-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Coop88||07-02-2001||03:39 AM||||When I wiggle the wheels, the only thing that moves is the tie rods. Not the ends, but the rods themselves. Maybe I screwed both sides up with that curb? There is no bushing movement. All is well there. Thanks to everyone who has replied so far.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Coop88 (edited 07-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||mrfiero||07-02-2001||01:42 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Coop88:<BR><B>When I wiggle the wheels, the only thing that moves is the tie rods. Not the ends, but the rods themselves. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>There might be some movement in the tie rods.......this is the slop or slack in the rack when the steering wheel is locked (if you try to turn the steering wheel when it is locked it will still move a little bit). <P>It sounds like it is some other problem.....something that I have no idea about without seeing the car (maybe you have a bad rack?).  I could still stop by on my trip out east......I can delievr that tailpipe part at the same time.  LMK.<P>   <p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 07-02-2001).]||||reg||1
